## 9. Human-only actions and approval gates

A coding agent must not invent or independently decide the following. Ask the human or leave an explicit action item when it is required:

- Creating/billing provider accounts; supplying real provider API keys; granting permissions to private agents/workspaces.
- Providing encryption/session/OIDC secrets, approving a secrets manager, or changing production key-rotation policy.
- Creating customer-owned PostgreSQL instances/users, authorizing network access, selecting a cloud/host, or approving external data access.
- Choosing retention/deletion policies, legal/privacy terms, budget ceilings, model/vendor policy, data residency, or customer-facing claims.
- Purchasing domains/certificates/services, changing DNS, production firewall/egress rules, or configuring billing/monitoring alerts.
- Approving destructive data deletion, irreversible migrations, schema/data cutovers, production rollout, or rollback.
- Performing the final live smoke test with actual customer/provider credentials and explicitly approving production release.

When a human action is needed, provide the exact decision/input required, why it is needed, safe setup instructions, and what remains blocked. Never request that the user paste secrets into chat or commit them to the repository.

## 10. Required end-of-phase report

At the end of each phase, report concisely:

1. Phase name and status: `COMPLETE` or `BLOCKED` (never imply a blocked phase is complete).
2. What changed and why; list changed files.
3. Exact test/build/lint commands and pass/fail/blocked results.
4. Commit hash and branch; state whether a remote push occurred or was unavailable.
5. Known limitations, unverified checks, and security risks.
6. Human-only actions, if any, and whether they block the next phase.

Be candid. “Not run” is not “passed.” A green unit-test suite is not production certification.
